"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 16.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3) and pathological stage pT3a, with positive surgical margins, but no seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, or lymph node metastasis documented. The biopsy grade (Gleason 4+3) matches the prostatectomy specimen.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymph node metastasis and seminal vesicle invasion. However, the positive surgical margins and the high preoperative PSA contribute to risk. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 41.66 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 31.24–52.07 months; its coverage has not been validated."
